"Packs individual observations perpendicular to their numeric axis while preserving exact measured positions.\n\nUse when: Individual observations and approximate local concentration both matter. Sample size is moderate enough for collision-aware packing.\n\nAvoid when: Dense samples require so much packing width that neighboring groups collide.\n\nVisual grammar: The numeric coordinate encodes the measured observation exactly. Perpendicular displacement prevents point collisions and loosely indicates concentration.\n\nBuild: Fix the quantitative scale and a constant marker diameter. Sort or pack marks using collision detection without altering measured positions. Reserve enough strip width for the largest swarm and label sample sizes.\n\nCheck: Check no marks overlap after the final display size is chosen. Verify packing never shifts observations along the measured axis. Confirm neighboring swarms remain within separate group bands.\n\nPitfalls: Swarm width depends on marker size and packing algorithm, so it is not a calibrated density scale. Use histograms or violins when packing no longer fits.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic exam scores for three classes with exact score positions and tightly packed nonoverlapping dots.\n\nAlternatives: Strip plot, Wilkinson dot plot, Violin plot."
